WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:06.955
Effectiveness in high stakes situations
often boils down to expert teaming and

00:00:06.955 --> 00:00:08.650
communication.

00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:12.400
Yet many teams missed the mark and
perform sub optimally.

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:15.166
Hello, I'm Matt Abrahams and

00:00:15.166 --> 00:00:19.710
I teach Strategic Communication at
Stanford Graduate School of Business.

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:23.212
Welcome to think fast talk smart,
the podcast.

00:00:23.212 --> 00:00:28.506
Today, I am thrilled to speak with Sarah
Singer, who is a professor of medicine

00:00:28.506 --> 00:00:34.150
at Stanford School of Medicine and
a professor by courtesy at the GSB.

00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:39.348
Sarah teaches the popular course leading
and managing health care organizations,

00:00:39.348 --> 00:00:43.340
innovation and
collaboration in high stakes savings.

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:45.850
Her research focuses on measuring and

00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:49.931
improving organizational culture,
learning, teamwork,

00:00:49.931 --> 00:00:54.330
patient safety, integrated patient
care and culture of health.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:58.310
Welcome, Sarah, thanks for being here.

00:00:58.310 --> 00:01:01.370
&gt;&gt; Thanks for having me,
I'm excited to speak with you.

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:03.500
&gt;&gt; Great, let's get started.

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:07.924
Your research addresses the impact of
organizational culture in learning on

00:01:07.924 --> 00:01:09.030
teamwork.

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:14.330
Can you share some insights into what
makes for high functioning teams?

00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:19.089
&gt;&gt; When I try to boil the lessons down I
say that high performing teams require

00:01:19.089 --> 00:01:22.210
cultures with three key characteristics.

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:27.180
So they need to be team oriented,
learning oriented, and systems oriented.

00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:32.832
By that, I mean first a team oriented
culture suggests that leaders need to

00:01:32.832 --> 00:01:35.282
create conditions that support teamwork,

00:01:35.282 --> 00:01:38.659
including strategies that
overcome professional silos and

00:01:38.659 --> 00:01:43.040
hierarchies which you find in spades and
healthcare organizations.

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:47.182
So for example, by establishing
clear roles and responsibilities and

00:01:47.182 --> 00:01:51.668
making sure that people have an understand
the shared goals of the group, and

00:01:51.668 --> 00:01:56.820
supportive practices like offering
team time so people can work together.

00:01:56.820 --> 00:02:01.478
The second key characteristic is a
learning culture, where team members learn

00:02:01.478 --> 00:02:06.342
from mistakes and experimentation so they
can adapt and change and innovate all key

00:02:06.342 --> 00:02:12.020
factors for not just healthcare settings,
but lots of organizational settings.

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:15.858
Learning requires leadership that
reinforces learning a supportive

00:02:15.858 --> 00:02:19.501
environment, including especially
psychological safety, but

00:02:19.501 --> 00:02:21.720
also an appreciation for differences.

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:25.721
When you're working with people of
lots of different backgrounds and

00:02:25.721 --> 00:02:31.130
openness to new ideas, these environments
need setting aside time for reflection.

00:02:31.130 --> 00:02:35.109
And a learning environment also
requires learning practices and

00:02:35.109 --> 00:02:38.869
processes that promote training,
knowledge acquisition,

00:02:38.869 --> 00:02:43.150
performance monitoring in order to
facilitate learning in the team.

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:47.650
And that third key characteristics
was a systems oriented culture.

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:49.642
And by systems orientation,

00:02:49.642 --> 00:02:54.300
I mean an orientation that keeps
people focus on whole systems.

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:59.089
And addresses problems across conditions
and across units and over time, so

00:02:59.089 --> 00:03:01.300
that we overcome fragmentation and

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:06.470
we integrate across boundaries in order I
think of the systems around the patient.

00:03:06.470 --> 00:03:08.490
So we're caring for the whole patients.

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:12.204
So there's a lot underlying these insights
but these are some of the essentials for

00:03:12.204 --> 00:03:13.820
high performing teams.

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:18.309
&gt;&gt; Wow, so it really sounds
like it's about taking time to

00:03:18.309 --> 00:03:22.894
define clear responsibilities
set up support in time for

00:03:22.894 --> 00:03:28.067
reflection and really look beyond
your specific focus or silo.

00:03:28.067 --> 00:03:32.943
I often think it's about people process
and infrastructure and it sounds like all

00:03:32.943 --> 00:03:37.740
three of those play out to helping
high functioning teams be successful.

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:42.051
&gt;&gt; Absolutely especially in the
accomplishment of what I was describing is

00:03:42.051 --> 00:03:45.657
that learning oriented culture,
you need the leadership,

00:03:45.657 --> 00:03:49.985
you need the environment, and
you need those processes and practices.

00:03:49.985 --> 00:03:54.146
And all of them need to be aligned
in a way that supports people's

00:03:54.146 --> 00:03:58.848
willingness to speak up, to discuss
mistakes, to raise questions, so

00:03:58.848 --> 00:04:01.255
that there is learning that occurs.

00:04:01.255 --> 00:04:04.680
And that kind of open
communication is essential.

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:08.114
&gt;&gt; Two decades ago before returning
to academics, I ran learning and

00:04:08.114 --> 00:04:10.567
development groups for
high tech companies and

00:04:10.567 --> 00:04:13.940
I know that you've done
some research in that area.

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:17.432
For example, you looked at how
people learn new tools and

00:04:17.432 --> 00:04:23.250
techniques when the situation changes and
existing expertise is no longer as useful.

00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:26.820
Can you share what you found and what
lessons we can take away when it comes to

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:28.589
learning new skills for ourselves?

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:34.618
&gt;&gt; Sure, so this is recent research that
I did with Kate Kellogg from MIT where we

00:04:34.618 --> 00:04:39.229
studied managers approaches to dealing
with this challenge of teaching new tools

00:04:39.229 --> 00:04:40.252
and techniques.

00:04:40.252 --> 00:04:44.514
And in this case we work with a large
healthcare system that wanted to introduce

00:04:44.514 --> 00:04:46.270
a series of new processes.

00:04:46.270 --> 00:04:50.729
So for example, standard patient rooming
was one of the processes and historically

00:04:50.729 --> 00:04:55.490
the process of bringing a patient into
an exam room was just all over the map.

00:04:55.490 --> 00:04:59.268
And the organization wanted
everyone to do it the same way and

00:04:59.268 --> 00:05:03.120
to document it in the electronic
medical record the same way so

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:06.390
that they could bill more accurately for
it.

00:05:06.390 --> 00:05:10.796
Another kind of standard process that they
were trying to implement were standards or

00:05:10.796 --> 00:05:14.958
standard processes, standard timeframes
and documentation requirements for

00:05:14.958 --> 00:05:18.444
following up with a patient who
visited the emergency department or

00:05:18.444 --> 00:05:20.630
had been discharged from a hospital.

00:05:20.630 --> 00:05:24.599
So all key things that organizations
needed to do more consistently in

00:05:24.599 --> 00:05:27.230
the your era of electronic records.

00:05:27.230 --> 00:05:31.439
So the managers in this case, recognized
that the newer and often younger workers

00:05:31.439 --> 00:05:35.450
were more agile learners and
less committed to the status quo.

00:05:35.450 --> 00:05:40.353
But the problem was that they were also
junior to the workers who had long tenure

00:05:40.353 --> 00:05:43.769
there as the more senior
workers considered those more

00:05:43.769 --> 00:05:47.610
junior workers as illegitimate
in the trainer role.

00:05:47.610 --> 00:05:53.286
So in those units where managers assigned
the new workers to be the trainers,

00:05:53.286 --> 00:05:55.557
there was a strong backlash and

00:05:55.557 --> 00:05:59.853
the new processes were taken
up by the workers generally.

00:05:59.853 --> 00:06:04.219
But in a few of the units what we saw is
that the managers rotated the trainer role

00:06:04.219 --> 00:06:06.748
giving everybody a chance
to be the trainer.

00:06:06.748 --> 00:06:10.451
And when the managers offered the promise
of this call of status mobility,

00:06:10.451 --> 00:06:14.509
the senior worker trainees were willing
to learn from the junior trainers because

00:06:14.509 --> 00:06:18.760
they knew that they would have the turn
to be the trainers and themselves later.

00:06:18.760 --> 00:06:23.286
So this trainee status mobility,
we described as a mechanism that managers

00:06:23.286 --> 00:06:27.830
can use when they need to teach
more tenured workers new skills.

00:06:27.830 --> 00:06:30.687
&gt;&gt; Wow, and so that goes back to that
point of psychological safety that

00:06:30.687 --> 00:06:31.930
you also mentioned earlier.

00:06:31.930 --> 00:06:34.600
And that's a topic that's come
up before on this podcast.

00:06:34.600 --> 00:06:37.021
So By helping people
feel more comfortable,

00:06:37.021 --> 00:06:40.490
you're actually allowing them to learn and
grow.

00:06:40.490 --> 00:06:41.190
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:06:41.190 --> 00:06:45.871
And I'm not surprised that psychological
safety has come up before on this

00:06:45.871 --> 00:06:50.698
podcast because it is so critical for
the kind of learning that needs to happen

00:06:50.698 --> 00:06:54.060
on any team that wants to adapt or
change or innovate.

00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:59.133
So making people feel comfortable,
to be a learner at any state,

00:06:59.133 --> 00:07:02.340
leaders need to learn all the time too.

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:04.907
And so making people comfortable and

00:07:04.907 --> 00:07:09.957
confident that they can reach out with
questions or raise concerns and to

00:07:09.957 --> 00:07:16.320
just be a learner is something that every
team needs in order to be successful.

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:20.588
&gt;&gt; So true, and I'm hoping that this
actual podcast serves some of that need to

00:07:20.588 --> 00:07:22.750
help people learn more.

00:07:22.750 --> 00:07:25.470
Let me ask another question about
another one of your projects.

00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:30.023
In that project you noted that
high functioning healthcare teams

00:07:30.023 --> 00:07:34.590
simultaneously focus on functional and
cultural change.

00:07:34.590 --> 00:07:38.465
Can you define these two types of change
and share why addressing both is so

00:07:38.465 --> 00:07:39.476
helpful to teams?

00:07:39.476 --> 00:07:44.165
&gt;&gt; Yes certainly, so in this project
I studied primary care practices

00:07:44.165 --> 00:07:47.735
working to transform the way
they delivered care.

00:07:47.735 --> 00:07:48.450
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:07:48.450 --> 00:07:52.560
&gt;&gt; Moving from doctors is the focus of
the work to team based care where doctors

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:57.250
work with a medical assistant a nurse
maybe a social worker and others to dress.

00:07:57.250 --> 00:08:01.190
The whole holistic needs of the patients.

00:08:01.190 --> 00:08:05.090
So our question was, what made some
practices more successful than others at

00:08:05.090 --> 00:08:07.840
making this transition to team based care?

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:12.800
And what we found was that
the teams use different approaches.

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:15.836
So one form of approach was
a functional approach and

00:08:15.836 --> 00:08:17.906
the other was a cultural approach.

00:08:17.906 --> 00:08:21.099
And so these are sort of the way
that primary care practices

00:08:21.099 --> 00:08:22.871
approached effecting change.

00:08:22.871 --> 00:08:27.298
And by the functional approach we mean
an approach that focused on the practical

00:08:27.298 --> 00:08:31.197
operational aspects of teaming things
like making sure that there was

00:08:31.197 --> 00:08:34.104
clear role definition,
supportive structures and

00:08:34.104 --> 00:08:37.957
ensuring shared access to clinical
data all critically important.

00:08:37.957 --> 00:08:39.967
But that the cultural approach,

00:08:39.967 --> 00:08:43.914
in contrast was an approach that
addressed the more normative and

00:08:43.914 --> 00:08:48.293
relational aspects of teaming including
things like making sure that,

00:08:48.293 --> 00:08:53.173
the hierarchy was flattened sufficiently
so that everybody felt like they could

00:08:53.173 --> 00:08:58.371
provide some leadership and that there was
openness to experimentation on the team.

00:08:58.371 --> 00:09:03.057
What we found was that the groups that
pursued one or the other didn't do as well

00:09:03.057 --> 00:09:07.920
as the groups that pursued both,
that these were mutually reinforcing.

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:14.450
So for example, to successfully, establish
new roles, which was a functional goal.

00:09:14.450 --> 00:09:17.982
That required sharing authority and
flattening traditional hierarchies,

00:09:17.982 --> 00:09:20.860
which were both critical
aspects of culture.

00:09:20.860 --> 00:09:25.216
And there was also this recursive
relationship between the two approaches in

00:09:25.216 --> 00:09:29.438
that, the cultural changes created
an environment that were conducive to

00:09:29.438 --> 00:09:32.721
the functional change and
that functional changes then,

00:09:32.721 --> 00:09:35.890
in return supported the systems for
cultural changes.

00:09:35.890 --> 00:09:40.791
So, by undertaking both simultaneously,
a practice site could create a positive

00:09:40.791 --> 00:09:45.490
upward spiral that just got them better
and better at making the change.

00:09:45.490 --> 00:09:49.798
So primary care practices that prioritize
both types of change processes and

00:09:49.798 --> 00:09:52.922
pursued them simultaneously
were the most effective.

00:09:52.922 --> 00:09:56.814
&gt;&gt; Wow I can see how that could help,
not just in primary care, but

00:09:56.814 --> 00:09:58.293
in all organizations.

00:09:58.293 --> 00:10:02.628
And in my mind, as you were describing it,
I don't know why this happened, but

00:10:02.628 --> 00:10:06.578
I flashed to a yin yang symbol where
both parts blend together to bring out

00:10:06.578 --> 00:10:07.248
the whole.

00:10:07.248 --> 00:10:09.663
And it sounds like both a functional and

00:10:09.663 --> 00:10:13.950
cultural approach are really
critical to being successful.

00:10:13.950 --> 00:10:18.317
Now you know Sarah that this podcast
really focuses on communication.

00:10:18.317 --> 00:10:23.154
So I have to ask you what role does
communication play in effective teamwork

00:10:23.154 --> 00:10:28.142
and are there any best practices that you
suggest that can help all of us be more

00:10:28.142 --> 00:10:29.940
effective in our teaming.

00:10:29.940 --> 00:10:32.784
&gt;&gt; Absolutely,
communication plays a critical role,

00:10:32.784 --> 00:10:36.900
especially in situations that are
stressful where people are under duress.

00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:40.771
So think about the critical
moments in a surgical case, or

00:10:40.771 --> 00:10:44.090
when a patient is experiencing
a cardiac arrest.

00:10:44.090 --> 00:10:48.650
There are all sorts of critical moments
in healthcare where communication is key.

00:10:48.650 --> 00:10:53.460
One best practice, for example, that we
advocate is closed loop communication.

00:10:53.460 --> 00:10:57.146
Where one person says something and
another person repeats it back just to

00:10:57.146 --> 00:11:00.390
make sure that they've gotten
the information correct.

00:11:00.390 --> 00:11:03.143
So you say, we're ready for bypass and

00:11:03.143 --> 00:11:07.408
the person who responds I'm
putting the patient on bypass.

00:11:07.408 --> 00:11:13.293
Administer 10 cc of X Medication I'm
administering 10 ccs of X communication

00:11:13.293 --> 00:11:19.107
just to make sure that that communication
is received in the way it was intended.

00:11:19.107 --> 00:11:24.960
Another example is restructured forms of
communication and there are lots of them.

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:30.072
I spent a tremendous amount of time trying
to promote the use of surgical checklists,

00:11:30.072 --> 00:11:34.545
which have been made famous by Atul
Gawande, who is a colleague of mine from

00:11:34.545 --> 00:11:37.823
Harvard and
we work together on trying to spread this.

00:11:37.823 --> 00:11:42.576
And I was responsible for the evaluation
of the implementation of that work.

00:11:42.576 --> 00:11:46.858
But the basic idea is that
misunderstandings are possible and

00:11:46.858 --> 00:11:52.220
especially where there is hierarchy
information doesn't often transmit.

00:11:52.220 --> 00:11:57.456
And so making sure that you have
information kind of listed out

00:11:57.456 --> 00:12:02.492
that you know you need to review and
that encourages not just

00:12:02.492 --> 00:12:08.432
kind of the checks of the things that
you need to make sure that happen,

00:12:08.432 --> 00:12:12.965
but also to prompt discussion
including an important

00:12:12.965 --> 00:12:17.020
prompt that says,
I can't do this case alone.

00:12:17.020 --> 00:12:18.620
I need you to speak up.

00:12:18.620 --> 00:12:21.745
What if you see a problem so
extending that invitation and

00:12:21.745 --> 00:12:24.800
creating the psychological
safety in the space.

00:12:24.800 --> 00:12:27.279
Those forms of structured communication so

00:12:27.279 --> 00:12:30.970
that it happens reliably every
time can be a critically helpful.

00:12:32.230 --> 00:12:34.150
&gt;&gt; So much in what you just said.

00:12:34.150 --> 00:12:39.236
So the first part about inviting in
almost requiring that instant response

00:12:39.236 --> 00:12:44.090
increases what I call the F word of
communication and that is fidelity.

00:12:44.090 --> 00:12:47.950
The accuracy and
clarity by building in that repetition.

00:12:47.950 --> 00:12:52.597
You actually confirm that the other person
heard what you were saying there's In

00:12:52.597 --> 00:12:57.179
a very simplistic model of communication
called the transactional model where

00:12:57.179 --> 00:13:00.548
a sender sends a message to
a receiver who gives feedback.

00:13:00.548 --> 00:13:03.412
And insisting on that
external verbal response,

00:13:03.412 --> 00:13:07.856
you really do highlight fidelity and
reduce the noise that can get in the way.

00:13:07.856 --> 00:13:12.418
And the point of checklists and
having as part of those checklists,

00:13:12.418 --> 00:13:17.938
the structure the outline having part of
it that It demands that others involve and

00:13:17.938 --> 00:13:18.990
collaborate.

00:13:18.990 --> 00:13:23.960
I think it's so important we see in best
practices around how people actually meet.

00:13:23.960 --> 00:13:27.260
So teaming often occurs in meetings.

00:13:27.260 --> 00:13:29.977
And having those explicit
rules that everybody sees and

00:13:29.977 --> 00:13:33.212
you go through those steps can be
very helpful to being effective,

00:13:33.212 --> 00:13:35.830
efficient and
making sure that you achieve the goal.

00:13:35.830 --> 00:13:40.470
So really helpful ideas for
communication there.

00:13:40.470 --> 00:13:44.637
So I'm wondering if you have any last
thoughts or best practices that you'd like

00:13:44.637 --> 00:13:48.550
to share with us on how we can be
better in the teams we participate in?

00:13:48.550 --> 00:13:51.263
&gt;&gt; Let's see, so I can think of a couple.

00:13:51.263 --> 00:13:53.997
&gt;&gt; Sure.
&gt;&gt; I guess one is to make sure that

00:13:53.997 --> 00:13:59.543
the work that you are asking a team to
do actually requires a team to do it.

00:13:59.543 --> 00:14:04.630
We forget that lots of work doesn't
actually benefit from teams.

00:14:04.630 --> 00:14:09.637
If a work is interdependent then of course
it benefits from a team but if it's not

00:14:09.637 --> 00:14:14.819
if individuals can work independently then
I say let the individuals do the work.

00:14:14.819 --> 00:14:18.264
And avoid all the challenges
associated with teamwork and

00:14:18.264 --> 00:14:22.209
the requirements of communication
because that's really hard.

00:14:22.209 --> 00:14:26.997
And there are all sorts of ways in which
we are, we fail to communicate and so

00:14:26.997 --> 00:14:31.785
we suffer those process losses and
teams don't necessarily do better than

00:14:31.785 --> 00:14:35.675
than individuals so
I say make sure the work requires a team.

00:14:35.675 --> 00:14:40.355
And then secondly I'd say when work
does require a team make sure everybody

00:14:40.355 --> 00:14:45.122
fully understands, and is on board with
what the team needs to accomplish.

00:14:45.122 --> 00:14:48.107
So when they are, they're more
likely to do the right things and

00:14:48.107 --> 00:14:50.670
sometimes they'll do amazing things.

00:14:50.670 --> 00:14:55.385
A very small example of this that
made me think of it is, yesterday,

00:14:55.385 --> 00:15:00.268
I met with my research grant manager
to discuss the budget I was pulling

00:15:00.268 --> 00:15:04.489
together for a new research
study that I'm proposing and I,

00:15:04.489 --> 00:15:08.490
I said at the end we were
pulling things together.

00:15:08.490 --> 00:15:13.000
I said, so do you have any
questions about the project itself?

00:15:13.000 --> 00:15:15.770
And I realized that they knew
nothing about the project.

00:15:15.770 --> 00:15:20.868
And so I explained the project, and
then they poured out with suggestions for

00:15:20.868 --> 00:15:26.125
how to revise the budget to make it more
appropriate so that the funder would look

00:15:26.125 --> 00:15:31.860
at it and say no, they've really given
this some thought it was so helpful to me.

00:15:31.860 --> 00:15:34.430
And I learned that lesson over and
over again.

00:15:34.430 --> 00:15:39.328
Everybody on the team has something to
contribute, and they can only contribute

00:15:39.328 --> 00:15:43.690
it if they are on board with what
the team is trying to accomplish.

00:15:43.690 --> 00:15:46.225
Especially the people
who are lower status and

00:15:46.225 --> 00:15:48.955
we don't necessarily you
know have presidents in

00:15:48.955 --> 00:15:53.506
all of the meetings they're important too
&gt;&gt; Absolutely that's a double A answer you

00:15:53.506 --> 00:15:56.627
first need to make sure me that
teaming is appropriate and

00:15:56.627 --> 00:15:59.693
then you need to make sure
that everybody's aligned So

00:15:59.693 --> 00:16:03.531
the the appropriateness of meeting and
working in teams is the first question and

00:16:03.531 --> 00:16:06.140
then you have to make sure
that everybody's aligned.

00:16:06.140 --> 00:16:07.317
So thank you for that.

00:16:07.317 --> 00:16:08.440
&gt;&gt; Of that.

00:16:08.440 --> 00:16:12.920
&gt;&gt; So before we end, I like to ask
everybody the same three questions.

00:16:12.920 --> 00:16:15.141
Are you up for answering them?

00:16:15.141 --> 00:16:16.029
&gt;&gt; So I'll try.

00:16:16.029 --> 00:16:18.680
&gt;&gt; All right, question number one.

00:16:18.680 --> 00:16:23.754
If you were to capture the best
communication advice you ever received

00:16:23.754 --> 00:16:28.484
as a five to seven word presentation
slide title what would it be?

00:16:28.484 --> 00:16:31.240
&gt;&gt; Inspire change by
telling great stories.

00:16:31.240 --> 00:16:34.476
&gt;&gt; I love that so
I like the inspiration part of that and

00:16:34.476 --> 00:16:39.239
we've certainly heard across the many
podcasts the value that story has.

00:16:39.239 --> 00:16:42.820
Bullet points lists that's hard for
us to understand and

00:16:42.820 --> 00:16:46.260
take with us but
a good story is something that sticks.

00:16:46.260 --> 00:16:50.413
So thank you for reminding about
us how a story can inspire.

00:16:50.413 --> 00:16:52.632
&gt;&gt; I have to remind myself
of that all the time.

00:16:52.632 --> 00:16:54.630
&gt;&gt; Right, exactly.

00:16:54.630 --> 00:16:59.530
So question number two, who is
a communicator that you admire and why?

00:16:59.530 --> 00:17:05.325
&gt;&gt; Nick Kristof and maybe because I was
thinking of the New York Times, but I

00:17:05.325 --> 00:17:12.960
really admire the way he tells stories of
real people experiencing real suffering.

00:17:12.960 --> 00:17:20.260
He clearly believes we need to hear these
stories in order to affect change and

00:17:20.260 --> 00:17:24.769
I think he's brave at
hearing the hard things.

00:17:24.769 --> 00:17:27.710
I think it's not easy to hear them and
to bring them to light and

00:17:27.710 --> 00:17:30.214
share them with other people
in the way that he does.

00:17:30.214 --> 00:17:33.130
I find that really tough and admirable.

00:17:33.130 --> 00:17:37.890
&gt;&gt; What are the three first
ingredients that go into

00:17:37.890 --> 00:17:41.615
a successful communication recipe?

00:17:41.615 --> 00:17:46.070
&gt;&gt; I have some fear trepidation to speak
this to someone who teaches a course in

00:17:46.070 --> 00:17:49.920
strategic communication, but
I'll give it a shot though.

00:17:49.920 --> 00:17:53.464
So the ones for
me I think are a message and

00:17:53.464 --> 00:17:59.524
important message that you find
meaningful that you want to convey.

00:17:59.524 --> 00:18:04.234
And the second is a connection, so
thinking about how does your message

00:18:04.234 --> 00:18:08.300
relate to the people that
you're trying to reach?

00:18:08.300 --> 00:18:11.839
And the third,
as we've been talking about,

00:18:11.839 --> 00:18:15.670
is a story that moves people to agree or
to action.

00:18:15.670 --> 00:18:20.306
So, climate change is a classic
example of this reform advocates first

00:18:20.306 --> 00:18:24.882
talked about polar bears losing their
ice and it was less compelling.

00:18:24.882 --> 00:18:28.139
Then the kind of message that
you're hearing now, which is about,

00:18:28.139 --> 00:18:31.856
this is critical for the survival of
your children and your grandchildren and

00:18:31.856 --> 00:18:34.150
we need to do something about it.

00:18:34.150 --> 00:18:39.930
And the most compelling storyteller I
know in this genre is David Attenborough.

00:18:39.930 --> 00:18:44.220
The one I like most recently was
a show called A Life on Our Planet,

00:18:44.220 --> 00:18:49.950
which if you haven't seen it you really
must but quintessential storyteller.

00:18:49.950 --> 00:18:54.616
And in my field the really compelling
stories tend to be about opportunities for

00:18:54.616 --> 00:18:57.201
teams to save lives, or little lives lost.

00:18:57.201 --> 00:19:00.090
More often we hear about lives
loss because teams have failed.

00:19:00.090 --> 00:19:05.046
And they're often about ways that leaders
failed to establish psychological safety

00:19:05.046 --> 00:19:07.798
so teams don't achieve
their full potential or

00:19:07.798 --> 00:19:10.160
about lack of reliably safe processes.

00:19:10.160 --> 00:19:14.077
So cardiovascular surgeon who shuts
down a conversation in the surgical room

00:19:14.077 --> 00:19:16.849
by berating his trainees and
people don't speak up and

00:19:16.849 --> 00:19:19.272
something terrible
happens to their patient.

00:19:19.272 --> 00:19:23.303
Or the primary care team that
fumbles positive test result and

00:19:23.303 --> 00:19:27.580
doesn't act when they have
information that they should act on.

00:19:27.580 --> 00:19:30.895
And they don't catch the patient's
colorectal cancer until it's

00:19:30.895 --> 00:19:33.460
progressed beyond the possibility for
treatment.

00:19:33.460 --> 00:19:37.512
So super sad stories that
are calls to action and

00:19:37.512 --> 00:19:42.879
we use them because we need people
to make make processes better

00:19:42.879 --> 00:19:47.760
make communication better for
patients going forward.

00:19:48.780 --> 00:19:53.408
&gt;&gt; Well you had nothing to worry about
in terms of your strategic communication

00:19:53.408 --> 00:19:57.469
abilities you those three
ingredients are absolutely critical.

00:19:57.469 --> 00:20:02.755
And can definitely be helpful not just
in a healthcare setting but beyond.

00:20:02.755 --> 00:20:04.735
So Sarah, thank you so much.

00:20:04.735 --> 00:20:07.036
Your ideas and insights into teamwork and

00:20:07.036 --> 00:20:11.235
training and communication
are incredibly useful and actionable.

00:20:11.235 --> 00:20:14.022
I appreciate your time and your insight.

00:20:14.022 --> 00:20:18.410
&gt;&gt; Thank you I appreciate having
the opportunity to speak with you today.

